# rhythm-kiosk :: install to /etc/sudoers.d/010_kiosk-nopasswd
#
# Install with (mode 0440, root:root -- sudo REFUSES group/world-writable
# sudoers files and ignores files whose names contain a '.' or end in '~'):
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0440 \
#        ~/rhythm-kiosk-staging/010_kiosk-nopasswd /etc/sudoers.d/010_kiosk-nopasswd
#   sudo visudo -c        # MUST print "parsed OK" before you log out
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# The mapping editor (/opt/rhythm-kiosk/bin/mapping-editor.sh) writes
# root-owned files under /opt/rhythm-kiosk (games.conf, controllers.conf,
# games/<id>/ payloads). The kiosk is a TV-attached appliance driven by a
# controller and an occasional keyboard: a hidden sudo password prompt inside
# a throwaway xterm is exactly the kind of silent stall this project bans.
# With this drop-in the editor never prompts; without it the editor's
# `sudo -n true` pre-flight fails LOUDLY and names this file.
#
# PRECEDENT: this is the RetroPie pattern -- RetroPie ships
# /etc/sudoers.d/010_pi-nopasswd containing `pi ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL`.
# Same file-number prefix, same one-line grant, same purpose (an appliance
# whose maintenance UI must never block on a password prompt).
#
# SSH CAVEAT
# This makes the kiosk account's password the only thing between a remote
# login and root. If the SSH server is enabled on this box, use KEY
# AUTHENTICATION and disable password auth:
#   PasswordAuthentication no
# in /etc/ssh/sshd_config (then `sudo systemctl restart ssh`).
# If SSH is not installed -- the default for this build -- physical access is
# already root access and this changes nothing.
#
# The boot-into-game path uses NO sudo at all; this grant exists only for
# maintenance mode.

kiosk ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
